### 5. Subagent execution and ownership

Use a small set of focused subagents. Begin with three workers after the lead has fixed shared contracts; split Drive and Confluence into separate workers only if that is a useful parallel boundary. Normally cap concurrent workers at four plus the lead.

| Owner | Owns | Must not edit independently |
|---|---|---|
| Lead | Shared contracts, schema/migrations, root dependencies, model gateway, retrieval runner, audit/OTel integration, startup, final acceptance | Other workers' active files without coordinating |
| Identity worker | Application sessions, role checks, source-account linking, credential references, authorization service | Shared schema/contracts/root lockfiles |
| Connector worker | Google Drive and Confluence adapters, extraction and provider error mapping | Platform identity logic, central authorization rules |
| Frontend worker | Application shell, sign-in, connection screens, chat, source panel and simple Activity view | Backend contracts or authentication semantics |

Adapt paths to the inspected repo and record ownership before delegation. The lead alone edits common migrations and root dependency/lockfiles; workers request required additions in batches. Use isolated worktrees when appropriate and supported, otherwise use non-overlapping directory ownership. Do not modify global model/config settings to force subagent availability.

Suggested sequence:
1. Lead defines domain/API/event contracts, setup and a source/model-access checklist.
2. Workers implement independent modules against those contracts while the lead implements model, retrieval and observability foundations.
3. Integrate one real source through the entire app early, then the second.
4. Reassign a completed worker for read-only review of authorization, secret handling and ownership boundaries. The reviewer writes no tests and delegates no broad redesign.
5. Lead operates the integrated UI and APIs, fixes discovered issues, records evidence and hands over.

Every worker inherits the no-test rule and iteration boundary. Reports should name changed files, contract assumptions, observed checks and blockers. The lead owns the final result; do not treat worker completion messages as proof of integration.

If subagent tooling is unavailable, disclose that limitation and continue the same scoped work sequentially.

